---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ArticleHero from '../../components/ArticleHero.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="Hair Extensions Guide: Methods, Upkeep & Questions to Ask"
  description="An overview of common hair extension methods, how they're maintained, and what to ask before booking."
  canonicalPath="/hair-services/hair-extensions/"
>
  <ArticleHero eyebrow="Hair Services" title="Hair Extensions: What to Know" image="/images/gallery/gallery-private-studio-2.jpg" imageAlt="Private salon studio setup" />
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
    <p class="text-xs text-ink/50 mb-2"><a href="/" class="hover:underline">Home</a> / <a href="/hair-services/" class="hover:underline">Hair Services</a> / Hair Extensions</p>

    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Extensions add length, volume, or both, but the method matters as much as the
      hair itself. Different application techniques suit different hair types,
      budgets, and lifestyles, and choosing the wrong one for your situation is the
      most common reason people end up unhappy with the result. This guide covers
      the main methods, what upkeep actually looks like, and the questions worth
      asking before you commit.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Common Methods</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-ink/80 mb-6">
      <li><strong>Tape-in:</strong> thin wefts adhered with medical-grade tape, typically reset every 6–8 weeks. Popular for being relatively fast to apply and gentle on fine hair.</li>
      <li><strong>Sew-in / weft:</strong> wefts sewn onto braided tracks, common for adding significant length or volume. Requires enough natural hair density to braid a secure base.</li>
      <li><strong>Clip-in:</strong> temporary, removable extensions you apply and remove yourself, ideal for occasional wear without a salon appointment.</li>
      <li><strong>Fusion / keratin bond:</strong> individual strands bonded with heat, longer-lasting but more involved to apply and remove — often the most natural-looking option but also the most time-intensive.</li>
      <li><strong>Micro-link / beaded:</strong> strands attached with small metal beads rather than heat or tape, which some people prefer to avoid heat-based bonding altogether.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Matching Method to Hair Type</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Fine or thin hair generally does best with lighter-weight methods like
      tape-ins, since heavier techniques can put more tension on individual strands
      and increase breakage risk. Thicker, more resilient hair has more flexibility
      to carry sew-in wefts or fusion bonds without as much strain. If you have a
      history of hair breakage or a sensitive scalp, mention that during your
      consultation — an experienced stylist will often steer you toward a gentler
      method even if it's not the one you originally asked about.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Upkeep</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Most methods require periodic salon visits to move or replace the extensions
      as your natural hair grows, along with gentle brushing — starting from the
      ends and working up — and sulfate-free products at home. Sleeping with hair
      loosely braided can help reduce tangling and matting around the attachment
      points overnight, which is one of the most common sources of frustration with
      extensions in the first few weeks.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">What Extensions Cost Over Time</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      It's worth thinking about extensions as an ongoing cost, not a one-time
      purchase. Beyond the initial application, most methods require maintenance
      appointments every 6–10 weeks, and the hair itself may eventually need to be
      replaced or supplemented depending on the quality and how well it's cared
      for. Ask your stylist for a realistic estimate of the total cost over six
      months to a year, not just the price of the first appointment.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Questions to Ask Before Booking</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-ink/80 mb-8">
      <li>Which method do you recommend for my hair density, texture, and goals?</li>
      <li>What's the expected maintenance schedule and cost over time?</li>
      <li>What hair quality and source do you use, and can I see samples?</li>
      <li>How should I sleep, wash, and style my hair differently once extensions are in?</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Signs It's Time to Remove Extensions</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Extensions shouldn't be left in indefinitely regardless of method — most
      manufacturers and stylists recommend a maximum wear time before removal and
      reapplication, both to protect your natural hair and because the
      attachment points naturally loosen as hair grows. Matting near the roots,
      visible slippage, or noticeable tension or discomfort are all signs it's
      time for a removal and reassessment rather than pushing past the
      recommended timeline.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Removal Should Also Be Professional</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      It's worth booking removal with a professional rather than attempting it
      yourself, especially for bonded or taped methods — the solvents and
      techniques used to safely release adhesive or bonds are specifically
      designed to avoid pulling on natural hair, and DIY removal is one of the
      more common ways people end up with unexpected breakage or damage from an
      otherwise well-maintained set of extensions.
    </p>

    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
      See also: <a href="/tools/salon-cost-calculator/" class="text-rose-600 underline">Salon Cost Calculator</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
